# Normalize line endings in the repository, and check them out natively.
* text=auto

# ...except these two, which are data rather than source.
#
# A brain key or a BIP-39 mnemonic is only portable if every implementation sees
# exactly the same words in exactly the same order, so the tests pin both files
# by SHA-256. Git's default CRLF conversion on Windows changes those bytes and
# the pin fails — which is how this was found, on the first Windows CI run.
#
# `.lines()` in Rust strips a trailing \r, so word *lookup* would have survived
# the conversion. The pin is the stricter guarantee and it is the one that
# matters: these files must be byte-identical to beem's, everywhere, forever.
# Marking them binary means git never rewrites them on any platform.
hivecomb/data/brainkey_words.txt -text
hivecomb/data/bip39_english.txt  -text

# Captured node responses, compared as parsed JSON but kept verbatim so a
# regenerated fixture diffs cleanly against the one it replaces.
hivecomb/tests/fixtures/*.json -text
